<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-calculus">
  <title>Newton, Leibniz, and the Calculus (8.2-8.4)</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Forerunners-of-Calculus">
    <title>Forerunners of Calculus</title>

    <p>
      Calculus emerged in the 17th century with the discovery that two of the most well known problems of mathematics at the time, the area problem and the tangent line problem are essentially inverses of each other. During the 16th and 17th centuries many mathematicians worked on one or both of these problems and some developed techniques for solving them, but when it comes to the discovery of calculus, there are three things that are required for a theory of calculus:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            A systemic method for finding areas.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A systemic method for finding tangents.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A profound connection between the area and tangent problems.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz independently came up with methods that satisfied all three requirements and thus they are considered the discoverers of calculus; however, they did not develop their ideas out of nothing, but were influenced by the mathematicians who had come before them. To better appreciate the magnitude of their discoveries, it is helpful to consider the state of mathematics in the 17th century before the discovery of calculus.
    </p>

    <p>
      The problem of finding the area of geometric shapes goes back to ancient times; however, in the 17th century new techniques were developed to calculate areas. A common technique was the <term>method of indivisibles</term> which was popularized by Bonaventura Cavalieri and is also known as <term>Cavalieri's principle</term>. In the two dimensional case the method of indivisibles states that if two regions of a plane are included between two parallel lines in that plane and if every line parallel to those lines intersects both regions in line segments of equal length than the two regions have equal areas. Cavalieri considered a 2D object such as a square to be made up of an infinite number of lines. If those lines were shifted up or down, but their lengths stayed the same then the resulting shape would have the same area as the square. The method of indivisibles can also be extended to volumes in which the lines between regions are replaced by planes whose cross sections have equal areas. Using these techniques Cavalieri was able to determine areas and volumes of many shapes.
    </p>

    <p>
      The method of indivisibles has some noticeable drawbacks. First off it is not entirely rigorous since it implies that a 2D shape is made up of an infinite number of lines with no area. This makes it very easy to obtain incorrect results. For these reasons the method of indivisibles was replaced with the <term>method of infinitessimals</term>. Whereas the indivisibles had dimension one less than the figure, infinitessimals were of the same dimension as the figure. For example instead of a square being made up as an infinite number of lines, in the method of infinitessimals it would be thought of as made up of infinitely many rectangles of infinitessimal width. While more rigorous than the method of indivisibles, the method of infinitessimals was not without flaws. In particular if a square is made of infinitely many rectangles and those rectangles all have positive area (no matter how small) then it would seem that the square must have infinite area. Mathematicians justified this by saying that the rectangles had width that was smaller than any positive quantity, but not zero. 
    </p>

    <p>
      A very different approach to the area problem was developed by the English mathematician John Wallis by 1676. Unlike the other approaches to the area problem, this one uses an arithmetic approach rather than a geometric one. We can illustrate Wallis' method by considering the area beneath the parabola <m>y = x^2</m> on the interval <m>[0,1]</m>. Wallis' approach is based off the following observations:
      <md>
        <mrow> \frac{0^2+1^2}{1^2+1^2} \amp = \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{6} </mrow>
        <mrow> \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2}{2^2+2^2+2^2} \amp = \frac{5}{12} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{12} </mrow>
         <mrow> \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2+3^2}{3^2+3^2+3^2+3^2} \amp = \frac{7}{18} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{18} </mrow>
         <mrow> \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2+3^2+4^2}{4^2+4^2+4^2+4^2+4^2} \amp = \frac{3}{8} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{24} </mrow>
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Wallis concluded that in general
      <md>
        \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2+3^2+ \ldots + n^2}{n^2+n^2+n^2+n^2+ \ldots + n^2} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{6n}
      </md>
      By taking <m>n</m> to have larger and larger values, the right hand side approaches the limiting value of <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      To determine the area under the parabola <m>y= x^2</m> from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=a</m>, Wallis considered the area under the parabola to be made up of <m>n</m> infinitely narrow vertical rectangles each with width <m>\frac{a}{n}</m>. The ratio of the sum of the areas of these rectangles to the rectangle cirumscribing the entire parabola is
      <md>
        \frac{(\frac{0a}{n})^2+(\frac{1a}{n})^2+(\frac{2a}{n})^2+ \ldots + (\frac{0a}{n})^2}{a^2+a^2+a^2+ \ldots + a^2} = \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2+\ldots + n^2}{n^2+n^2 +\ldots + n^2}
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Wallis thus concluded that the ratio of the area underneath the parabola to the area of the circumscribing rectangle was
      <md>
        \lim_{n\to \infty}  \frac{0^2+1^2+2^2+\ldots + n^2}{n^2+n^2 +\ldots + n^2} = \frac{1}{3}.
      </md>
      In modern notation this says that
      <md>
        \frac{\int_0^a x^2dx}{a^3} = \frac{1}{3}
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      After working out several special cases, Wallis concluded that 
      <md>
        \int_0^a x^k dx = \frac{a^{k+1}}{k+1}
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      The other important problem in 17th century European mathematics was finding tangent lines. Pierre de Fermat gave a formula for finding tangent lines that was based off his <term>adequality</term> method for finding the maximum or minimum of functions. As an illustration we demonstrate how this method is used to maximize a quantity. Suppose we want to divide a line segment <m>AC</m> into 2 pieces at a point <m>E</m> so that the product <m>|AE| \times |EC|</m> is maximized. Fermat described his solution of the problem as follows.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        We write <m>AC = b</m>; let <m>a</m> be one of the segments so that the other will be <m>b-a</m>, and the product, the maximum of which is to be found will be <m>ba-a^2</m>. Let now <m>a+e</m> be the first segment of <m>b</m>; the second will be <m>b-a-e</m>, and the product of the segments, <m>ba-a^2 +be -2ae-e^2</m>; this must be adequated with the preceding: <m>ba-a^2</m>. Suppressing common terms <m>be \sim 2ae + e^2</m>. Suppressing <m>e: b = 2a</m>. To solve the problem we must consequently take the half of <m>b</m>. 
      </p>

      <p>
        We can hardly expect a more general method.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Fermat's method is based off an assumption that the value of an expression doesn't change much if you are close to a maximum or minimum. Thus if the maximum occurs at <m>a</m> then <m>(b-a)\times a</m> must be close to <m> (b-(a-e)) \times (a+e)</m>. Expanding this out, this implies that <m>be</m> must be approximately equal to <m>2ae+e^2</m>. Dividing both sides by <m>e</m> gives that <m>b</m> is approximately <m>2a+e</m>. Since <m>e</m> needs to be sufficiently small for this to be true, we can take <m>e=0</m> and conclude that <m>b = 2a</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      As mentioned Fermat used a similar approach to determine the tangent line to an arbitrary point on a parabola. Rene Descartes developed a method of finding a normal line to a given point on a curve, from which a tangent line could be determined. He illustrated it with three examples that demonstrated how his method worked; however, in general his method is not easy to apply and does not work for all curves. A third method was developed by Gilles Personne de Roberval which relates the problem of finding a tangent line to a problem of finding the direction of motion at an instant. Ultimately, however, before Newton and Leibniz there was no general method for finding tangent lines, though there were several methods that could work in special cases.
    </p>



    
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Newton">
    <title>Newton and the Development of Calculus</title>

    <p>
      Newton built off the work of Wallis to investigate the area problem. Wallis had found the areas under certain curves such as <m>y=1</m>, <m>y = 1-x^2</m>, <m>y=(1-x^2)^2</m>. To find the area under a more complicated curve such as <m>y = \sqrt{1-x^2}</m>. This is done by noticing that these equations form a pattern <m>(1-x^2)^{0/2}</m>, <m>(1-x^2)^{1/2}</m>, <m>(1-x^2)^{2/2}</m>, <m>(1-x^2)^{3/2}</m>, <m>(1-x^2)^{4/2}</m>
    </p>

    <p>
      It follows from this that the area under the curve <m>(1-x^2)^{1/2}</m> lies between those of <m>y=1</m> and <m>y = 1-x^2</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Newton made the observation that the curve <m>y = (1-x^2)^{0/2} = 1</m> sits above an area of <m>x</m>, the curve <m>y = (1-x^2)^{2/2} = 1-x^2</m> sits above an area of <m>x-\frac{1}{3}x^3</m>, and the curve <m>y = (1-x^2)^{4/2} = 1-2x^2+x^4</m> sits above an area of <m>x - \frac{2}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{5}x^5</m>. Newton spotted the pattern and came up with the following rules for finding areas.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The general method which I had devised some time ago for measuring the quantity of curves by an infinite series of terms you have, in the following, rather briefly explained than narrowly demonstrated. To the base <m>AB</m> of some curve <m>AD</m> let the ordinate <m>BD</m> be perpendicular and let <m>AB</m> be called <m>x</m> and <m>BD</m> <m>y</m>. Let <m>a, b, c \ldots</m> be given quantities and <m>m,n</m> integers. Then
      </p>

      <p>
        Rule 1: If <m>ax^{m/n} = y</m>, then will <m>(na/(m+n))x^{(m+n)/n}</m> equal the area <m>ABD</m>. The matter will be evident by example. 
      </p>

      <p>
        Example 1: If <m>x^2=y</m>, that is, if <m>a = n = 1</m> and <m>m=2</m>, then <m>(\frac{1}{3})x^3 = ABD</m>. 
      </p>

      <p>
        Example 2: If <m>4\sqrt{x} = 4x^{1/2} = y</m>, then <m>\frac{8}{3}x^{3/2} = ABD</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        Example 4: If <m>\frac{1}{x^2} = x^{-2}</m>, that is, if <m>a = n = 1</m> and <m>m = -2</m> then <m>((1/-1)x^{-1}) = -x^{-1} = \alpha BD</m> infinitely extended in the direction of <m>\alpha</m>; the computation sets its sign negative because it lies on the further side of the line <m>BD</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Example 5: If <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2}} = y</m>, then <m>(\frac{2}{-1})x^{-1/2} = BD\alpha</m> 
      </p>

      <p>
        Example 6: If <m>(1/x) = y</m> then <m>(\frac{1}{0})x^{0/1} = (\frac{1}{0}) = infinity</m> just as the area of the hyperbola is on each side of the line <m>BD</m>. 
      </p>

    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Essentially Newton's Rule 1 states that the area under <m>y = ax^{m/n}</m> is <m>\frac{na}{n+m}x^{(n+m)/n}</m> provided that <m>\frac{m}{n} \neq -1</m>. As Example 6 shows the rule breaks down in this case. Newton also had a second rule for finding areas of curves that are combinations of curves of the kind that area computed with the first rule such as <m>x^2+x^{3/2}</m>. The area underneath such a curve is the sum of the areas underneath the simpler curves.  
    </p>

    <p>
      From Newton's writings, we know that he was familiar with Rule 1 by 1665. He had also developed a method for finding tangents that was similar to Fermat's adequality method. We will consider Newton's method applied to the curve <m>x^3+y^3 -3axy = 0</m>, which is known as the folium of Descartes. The problem is to find a tangent to this curve.
    </p>

    <p>
      To solve this problem, Newton let's <m>o</m> denote a very small quantity and argues that what is <m>(x,y)</m> at one instant will be <m>(x+p0, y+q0)</m> in the next. Thus
      <md>
        (x+po)^3 + (y+qo)^3 -3a(x+po)(y+qo) = 0.
      </md>
      Expanding the brackets gives that 

      <md>
        x^3+3x^2po + 3x(po)^2 + (po)^3 + y^3+3y^2qo + 3y(qo)^2 + (qo)^3 - 3axy - 3axqo - 3aypo - 3apoqo = 0
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Since the original equation is <m>x^3+y^3-3axy = 0</m>, Newton deduced that the terms with <m>o</m> in it must sum to <m>0</m>. Dividing those terms by <m>o</m> yields the equation
      <md>
        3x^2p + 3xp^2o +p^3o^2 + 3y^2q + 3yq^2o + q^3o^2 - 3axq - 3ayp - 3apqo = 0
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Since <m>o</m> is very small, Newton argued that the terms with <m>o</m> were negligible compared to the ones without <m>o</m>. Dropping those terms leaves
      <md>
        3x^2p + 3y^2q - 3axq - 3ayp = 0
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Finally the slope of the tangent will be <m>\frac{q}{p}</m> which in this case is 

      <md>
        \frac{q}{p} = \frac{x^2-ay}{ax-y^2}. 
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Newton's method for finding tangents works because the slope of the secant line from <m>(x,y)</m> to <m>(x+po, y+qo)</m> is <m>\frac{p}{q}</m>. When <m>o</m> is very small the secant line is approximately a tangent line.
    </p>

    <p>
      Newton formalized these ideas in a manuscript he wrote in 1671 called <em>The Methods of Series and Fluxions</em>. In it all variables are thought of as varying over time and are called <term>fluents</term>. The rate at which these variables change is called their <term>fluxions</term>. Newton used fluxions to justify his rules for finding tangent lines. His argument is that each fluent (<m>x</m> or <m>y</m>) increases during an infinitely small period of time by what he called its <term>moment</term>, which is proportional to the speed of flow. That is the moment is equal to <m>o\dot{x}</m> where <m>o</m> is a very small quantity and <m>\dot{x}</m> is its fluxion. 
    </p>

    <p>
      To find the tangent line to the curve <m>x^3+y^3-3axy = 0</m>, Newton replaced <m>x</m> with <m>x+o\dot{x}</m> and <m>y</m> with <m>y+o\dot{y}</m>. From there he expanded terms as previously done to find that 
      <md>
        \frac{\dot{y}}{\dot{x}} = \frac{x^2-ay}{ax-y^2}. 
      </md>
      and thus this is the slope of the tangent line.
    </p>

    <p>
      Newton also explored the connection between the area problem and the tangent problem. In his writings he considered a curve given by an unknown equation between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. The area underneath it is taken to be <m>z = \frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}</m>. Newton argued that if this is the area then the curve is <m>y = x^{1/2}</m>, which is precisely what you obtain when you apply his <m>o</m>-method to find the tangent through the curve <m>y = \frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}</m>. Newton had realized that the problem of finding the area under a curve is the inverse of the tangent problem. Newton's discovery is the basis of the modern Fundamental Theorem of Calculus; however, Newton viewed his result as more of a definition than a theorem. In Newton's mind if you wanted to find the area underneath a curve, you would look at a table of tangents until you found a tangent with the same equation as your curve. From there you could find the original function, which would give you the area under the curve. For example, if you wanted to find the area of <m>y = x^{1/2}</m>, you would guess that the area under the curve is <m>z = \frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}</m>. From there you can check your guess by finding the slope of the tangent at <m>x</m>, which is <m>y=x^{1/2}</m>. Since this is the equation we started with, it confirms that our guess is correct and that we have found the area under the curve.  
    </p>

    <p>
      Newton's writings show that his method could be used to find areas for a variety of curves, both algebraic and transcendental. The one advantage that Newton's approach had over previous methods was that it allowed more complicated tangents to be broken into sums of simpler tangents. Using modern notation, if Newton found that the tangent of <m>y = f(x)</m> at <m>a</m> was <m>f'(a)</m> and the tangent of <m>y = g(x)</m> at <m>a</m> was <m>g'(a)</m> then his methods would imply that the tangent of <m> y = f(x)+g(x)</m> at <m>a</m> was <m>f'(a)+g'(a)</m>.
    </p>

    

    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Leibniz">
    <title>Leibniz and Calculus</title>

    <p>
      Gottfried Leibniz was born in 1646 in Leipzig, Germany. His father was Professor of Moral Philosophy at the University of Leipzig and Gottfried attended himself when he was 14. He graduated at age 20 and began to make a career in various disciplines. In addition to Calculus he is noted for developing a library catalog system that was used by many of Europe's largest libraries, developing the binary number system, and his work in developing mechanical calculators. In philosophy he postulated that the actual world is <q>the best of all possible worlds</q>, which he used to explain the problem of evil. Based on this view Emil du Bois-Reynard wrote that Leibniz thought of God as a mathematician.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        As is well known, the theory of the maxima and minima of functions was indebted to him for the greatest progress through the discovery of the method of tangents. Well, he conceives God in the creation of the world like a mathematician who is solving a minimum problem, or rather, in our modern phraseology, a problem in the calculus of variations – the question being to determine among an infinite number of possible worlds, that for which the sum of necessary evil is a minimum.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      In 1672 he was invited to come to Paris by the French government. There he met Dutch physicist and mathematician Christian Huygens and realized that his own knowledge of mathematics was lacking. With Huygens as his mentor, Leibniz began to teach himself mathematics. Huygens challenged him with the following question: What is the sum of the series
      <md>
        \frac{1}{1} + \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{6} + \frac{1}{10} + \ldots
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Leibniz developed a method for summing series by taking differences. The idea is that given a series <m>b_1+b_2+b_3 + \ldots</m> if you can find a sequence <m>a_1, a_2, a_3, \ldots</m> such that <m>b_1=a_1-a_2</m>, <m>b_2=a_2-a_3</m>, <m>b_3 = a_3-a_4</m>, and so forth, then 
      <md>
        b_1+b_2+b_3+ b_n = (a_1-a_2)+(a_2-a_3) + (a_3-a_4) + \ldots (a_n-a_{n+1})
      </md>
      and most of these terms cancel out. If the series has a finite number of terms then the sum is <m>a_1-a_{n+1}</m> and if the series is infinite then the sum is <m>a_1</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Leibniz used his technique to solve Huygen's problem. He realized that <m>\frac{1}{1} = 2(1-\frac{1}{2})</m>, <m>\frac{1}{3} = 2(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{3})</m>. <m>\frac{1}{6} = 2(\frac{1}{3}- \frac{1}{4})</m> and so forth. Hence
      <md>
        \frac{1}{1} + \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{6} + \frac{1}{10} + \ldots = 2(1 - \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2}- \frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{3}-\frac{1}{4} + \ldots) = 2
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      This result was not knew, but it was something that Leibniz had figured out for himself. Moreover, to him it illustrated a general principle that making sums and taking differences are inverse processes of each other.
    </p>

    <p>
      Leibniz applied this principle to geometry by regarding the region under a curve as being made up of infinitely many vertical lines. Thus the area of the region is the sum of the lines and the difference between successive lines taken to be a tiny fixed distance apart measures the slope of the boundary curve. This suggested to Leibniz that the area problem and the tangent problem were inverses of each other.
    </p>

    <p>
      In 1675 Leibniz developed the ideas of calculus and the rules that governed them. In his personal notes from that year he used the integral sign for the first time. He continued to refine his ideas and the notation for them. In 1684 he published his paper <em>A new method for maxima, minima and tangents</em> in which he set down the rules of differential calculus. He described his work as
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        A new method for maxima and minima as well as tangents, which is neither impeded by fractional nor irrational quantities, and a remarkable type of calculus for them.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Leibniz begins his paper by defining differentials.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Let an axis <m>AX</m> and several curves such as <m>VV</m>, <m>WW</m>, <m>YY</m>, <m>ZZ</m> be given of which the ordinates <m>VX</m>, <m>WX</m>, <m>YX</m>, <m>ZX</m> perpendicular to the axis are called <m>v, w, y, z</m> respectively. The segment <m>AX</m>, cut off from the axis is called <m>x</m>. Let the tangents be <m>VB, WC, YD, ZE</m> intersecting the axis respectively at <m>B,C,D,E</m>. Now some straight line selected arbitrarily is called <m>dx</m> and the line which is to <m>d</m> as <m>v</m> (or <m>w</m>, or <m>y</m>, or <m>z</m>) is to <m>XB</m> (or <m>XC</m> or <m>XD</m>, or <m>XE</m>) is called <m>dv</m> (or <m>dw</m>, or <m>dy</m>, or <m>dz</m>), or the difference of these <m>v</m> (or <m>w</m> or <m>y</m> or <m>z</m>). Under these assumptions we have the following rules of the calculus. 
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Next Leibniz sets out the rules of differentials.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        If <m>a</m> is a given constant then <m>da = 0</m> and <m>d(ax) = adx</m>. If <m>y = v</m> (that is, if the ordinte of any curve <m>YY</m> is equal to any corresponding ordinate <m>VV</m>) then <m>dy = dv</m>.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      These are the familiars rule from calculus that the derivative of a constant is <m>0</m> and that the derivative of <m>y = x</m> is <m>\frac{dy}{dx} = 1</m>. However, observe that Leibniz is working with differentials, not derivatives. A differential represents a small change in a variable, while a derivative is a ratio of differentials. Next Leibniz gives the rules for addition and subtraction of differentials.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        If <m>z-y+w+x=v</m> then <m>d(z-y+w+x) = dv = dz-dy+dw+dx</m>
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Multiplication and division follow next.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        <m>d(xv) = xdv + vdx</m> or setting <m>y = xv</m>, <m>dy = xdv+vdx</m>. It is indifferent whether we take a formula such as <m>xv</m> or its replacing letter such as <m>y</m>. It is too be noted that <m>x</m> and <m>dx</m> are treated in this calculus in the same way as <m>y</m> and <m>dy</m>, or any other indeterminate letter with its difference. It is also to be noted that we cannot always move backward from a differential equation without some caution, something we will discuss elsewhere.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now division:
        <md>
          d \frac{v}{y} = \frac{\pm vdy \mp ydv}{yy}
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Leibniz introduced the rules for multiplication and division without giving an indication of how they might be proven. Next Leibniz discussed the signs of differentials and how they can be used to find maxima and minima
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The following should be kept well in mind about the signs. When in the calculus for a letter simply its differential is substituted, then the signs are preserved; for <m>z</m> we write <m>dz</m>, for <m>-z</m> we write <m>-dz</m>, as appears from the previously given rule for addition and subtraction. However, when it comes to an explanation of the values, that is, when the relation of <m>z</m> to <m>x</m> is considered, then we can decide whether <m>dz</m> is a positive quantity or less than zero. When the latter occurs then the tangent <m>ZE</m> is not directed toward <m>A</m>, but in the opposite direction, down from <m>X</m>. This happens when the ordinates <m>z</m> decrease with increasing <m>x</m>. And since the ordinates <m>v</m> sometimes increase and sometimes decrease, <m>dv</m> will sometimes be positive and sometimes be negative; in the first case the tangent <m>VB</m> is directed toward <m>A</m>, in the latter it is directed in the opposite sense. None of these case happen in the intermediate position at <m>M</m>, at the moment when <m>v</m> neither increases nor decreases but is stationary. Then <m>dv=0</m> and it does not matter whether the quantity is positive or negative, since <m>+0 = -0</m>. At this place, <m>v</m>, that is, the ordinate <m>LM</m> is <em>maximum</em> (or when the convexity is turned to the axis, <em>minimum</em>), and the tangent to the curve at <m>M</m> is directed neither in the direction from <m>X</m> up to <m>A</m> to approach the axis, nor down to the other side, but is parallel to the axis. When <m>dv</m> is infinite with respect to <m>dx</m>, then the tangent is perpendicular to the axis, that is, it is the ordinate itself. When <m>dv = dx</m>, then the tangent makes half a right angle with the axis. When with increasing ordinates <m>t</m>; its increments or differences <m>dv</m> also increase (that is, when <m>dv</m> is positive d<m>dv</m>, the difference of the differences, is also positive, and when <m>dv</m> is negative d <m>dv</m> is also negative), then the curve turns toward the axis its concavity, in the other case its convexity. Where the increment is maximum or minimum, or where the increments from decreasing turn into increasing, or the opposite, there is a point of inflection. Here <em>concavity</em> and <em>convexity</em> are interchanged, provided the ordinates too do not turn from increasing into decreasing, or the opposite, because then concavity or convexity would remain. However, it is impossible that the increments continue to increase or decrease, but the ordinates turn from increasing to decreasing, or the opposite. Hence a <em>point of inflection</em> occurs when d <m>dv = 0</m> while neither <m>v</m> nor <m>dv = 0</m>. The problem of finding inflection therefore has not, like that of finding a maximum, two equal roots but three. This all depends on the correct use of signs.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      In the previous paragraph Leibniz laid out the rules for finding minima, maxima, and inflection points that are still used in Calculus. In his paper he also set down the rules for the differential of powers and roots (power rule). Then he addresses the differences between his calculus and previous approaches to the tangent problem. 
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Knowing thus the <em>Algorithm</em> (as I may say) of this calculus, which I call <em>differential calculus</em>, all other differential equations can be solved by a common method. We can find maxima and minima as well as tangents without the necessity of removing fractions, irrationals, and other restrictions, as had to be done according to the methods that have been published hitherto. The demonstration of all this will be easy to one who is experienced in these matters and who considers the fact, until now not sufficiently explored, that <m>dx, dy, dv, dw, dz</m> can be taken proportional to the momentary differences, that is increments or decrements, of the corresponding <m>x,y,v,w,z</m>. To any given equation we can thus write its differential equation. This can be done by simply substituting for each term (that is, any part which through addition or subtraction contributes to the equation) its differential quantity. For any other quantity (not itself a term, but contributing to the formation of the term) we use its differential quantity to form the differential quantity of the term itself, not by simple substitution, but according to the prescribed Algorithm. The methods published before have no such transition. They mostly use a line such as <m>DX</m> or of similar kind, but not the line <m>dy</m> which is the fourth proportional to <m>DX</m>, <m>DY</m>, <m>dx</m> -- something quite confusing. From there they go on removing fractions and irrationals (in which undetermined quantities occur). It is clear that our method also covers transcendental curves -- those that cannot be reduced by algebraic computation, or have no particular degree -- and thus holds in a most general way without any particular and not always satisfied assumptions.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Two years later Leibniz published a second paper in which he laid out the rules of the integral calculus and presented his proof of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.
    </p>
    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Criticism">
    <title>Criticisms of Calculus</title>

    <p>
      In 1734 the philosopher and Anglican bishop George Berkeley criticized Newton's calculus in his book <em>The Analyst</em> (subtitled <em>A Discourse Addressed to an Infidel Mathematician: Wherein It Is Examined Whether the Object, Principles, and Inferences of the Modern Analysis Are More Distinctly Conceived, or More Evidently Deduced, Than Religious Mysteries and Points of Faith</em>).
    </p>

    <p>
      The <q>infidel mathematician</q> was most likely Edmund Halley, who was an atheist and had previously criticized religious faith as being unreasonable. Berkeley's book was motivated by theology, not mathematics. However, Berkeley had studied mathematics and raised several valid criticisms against 17th century calculus. Berkeley accepted that calculus was correct; however, he argued that calculus was built on precepts that could only be accepted through faith, just like religion.  
    </p>

    <p>
      Berkeley's main objection to the calculus of Newton (and Leibniz as well) was that he made use of infinitessimal quantities which were sometimes taken be to small positive amounts and other times were treated like <m>0</m>. An example of this occurs in Newton's approach to finding tangent lines with the quantity <m>o</m> which Newton used to denote a very small amount. At one point in his method, Newton divides an equation by <m>o</m>, treating it as a non-zero quantity, but later he removes all terms with <m>o</m>, thus treating <m>o</m> as if it were <m>0</m>. Berkeley argued that this contradiction showed that calculus was built on faulty premises. He claimed that this showed <q>how Error may bring forth Truth, though it cannot bring forth Science</q>. Thus, Berkeley argued that calculus was unscientific and its foundations were no less intuitive than those of religion. He famously wrote
    </p>

   <blockquote>
    <p>
      And what are these fluxions? The velocities of evanescent increments. And what are these same evanescent increments? They are neither finite quantities, nor quantities infinitely small, nor yet nothing. May we not call them ghosts of departed quantities?
    </p>
   </blockquote>

   <p>
    In response to Berkeley criticisms, a number of mathematicians wrote responses in defense of Newton. One of the first was Colin MacLaurin, whose 1742 book <em>Treatise of Fluxions</em> was written as a direct reply to Berkeley. Other mathematicians worked to address Berkeley's criticisms and put calculus on a rigorous foundation. In a 1823 calculus textbook Augustin Cauchy gave a definition of derivative that used the concepts of limits to handle Berkeley's objection. His definition is basically the same as the modern definition of the derivative. If <m>f(x) = x^2</m> then we would argue that <m>f'(x) = 2x</m> because
    <md>
      <mrow> f'(x) \amp = \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{(x+h)^2 - x^2}{h} </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{x^2 +2xh + h^2 - x^2}{h} </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{2xh + h^2}{h} </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = \lim_{h\to 0} 2x + h </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp = 2x</mrow>
    </md>
    
    In this way, the concept of the limit is used to address Berkeley's criticisms. Because <m>h</m> is close to but not <m>0</m> we are justified to divide by <m>h</m> and because we are taking a limit as <m>h \to 0</m> we are justified in concluding that <m>2x+h</m> approaches <m>2x</m> as <m>h</m> goes to <m>0</m>.

   </p>

   <p>
     During the 1960's Abraham Robinson developed the theory of nonstandard analysis which provides a rigorous definition of infinitesimals and allows for the derivative to be defined in the same spirit of Newton and Leibniz.
   </p>
    
  </subsection>

  </section>
